WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:08.720
Imagine an alternate world where dinosaurs&nbsp;
evolved intelligence comparable to humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:14.320
What might these intelligent dinosaurs look&nbsp;
like? What kind of technology, culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:20.320
and art might they produce? This speculative&nbsp;
timeline is the subject of “Dinosauroids,” a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.520
worldbuilding project conceptualized in part by&nbsp;
C.M. Kosemen — whose work you may know from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.520
sci-fi book “All Tomorrows,” or his speculative&nbsp;
paleontology book All Yesterdays — the latter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:37.200
of which I’ve got a video on elsewhere in the&nbsp;
archive. “Dinosauroids” also comes from Simon Roy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.160
an accomplished comic book artist and writer who&nbsp;
created much of the art you’ll see in this video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.200
These two are outstanding creators, and I have&nbsp;
links to where you can follow and support them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:53.040
in this video’s description. The “Dinosauroids”&nbsp;
project was born in part out of Kosemen’s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:01:00.080
Roy’s frustration with the work of paleontologist&nbsp;
Dale Russel, who in 1982 created this concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.760
of how a species like a Troodon might evolve&nbsp;
into an upright-walking, bipedal organism with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.920
human-like intelligence. While the depiction has&nbsp;
a retro vibe I find kind of entertaining, it’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:18.720
pretty old-fashioned and human-centric model of an&nbsp;
intelligent dinosaur. So, Kosemen and Roy created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.720
their own, more scientifically-viable models which&nbsp;
don’t look much like humans — but have all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.880
right ingredients for human-like intelligence. So,&nbsp;
for this entry into the archive, we’ll explore the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.800
incredibly detailed speculative world and history&nbsp;
Roy and Koseman created to go alongside these new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.000
dinosaurs. They even imagine—incredibly—the&nbsp;
art these creatures might produce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.040
which will get to later in the video. Let’s&nbsp;
begin. In the hypothetical alternate timeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:55.280
of “Dinosauroids,” there was no end-Cretaceous&nbsp;
asteroid impact — nor did any other cataclysm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:01.200
befall the non-avian dinosaurs. Kosemen and Roy&nbsp;
theorize that even without a mass extinction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.520
dinosaur groups would have kept evolving into&nbsp;
new, unfamiliar forms. For now, let’s focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:13.280
the Dinosauroids themselves, and explore how the&nbsp;
authors speculate they might have first evolved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.964
The species, they imagine, originated from&nbsp;
troodon-like ancestors, small therapods with long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.964 --> 00:02:19.840
fingers that theoretically could have evolved into&nbsp;
grasping digits. The development of their advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:26.800
intelligence and early use of tools likely began&nbsp;
slowly. While the image of a bird-like species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:33.520
using primitive spears might seem surprising to&nbsp;
some, various modern birds — in particular crows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.880
which inspired the look of the Dinosauroids —&nbsp;
are highly intelligent, and have indeed been seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:45.520
using simple tools and solving logical puzzles. In&nbsp;
this experiment from The University of Auckland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.560
you can see a crow using stones to gain&nbsp;
access to submerged food — showing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.760
impressive understanding of water displacement&nbsp;
in the process. So, the idea of a bird-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.280
dinosaur achieving advanced intelligence through&nbsp;
a similar foundation isn’t too outside the realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.880
of possibility. This image illustrates the&nbsp;
two species of intelligent dinosaurs of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.840
alternate earth which both diverged from&nbsp;
a Troodon-like ancestor. While the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.920
multiple intelligent species living on one planet&nbsp;
at the same time might seem surprising — it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.280
too far off from our own history with humans&nbsp;
and Neanderthals. The hypothetical scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.920
designations of the two Dinosaurroids are&nbsp;
Avisapiens sauro-theos and Avisapiens Tatar-icus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.440
And while both species make frequent use of their&nbsp;
beaks for tool use, they also seem to have complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:40.720
hands with dexterous fingers — a feature&nbsp;
that would allow them to make delicate tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:47.200
and evolve brain function analogous to a human’s.&nbsp;
Aside from the Dinosauroids, the authors designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.800
an extensive ecosystem of animals around the&nbsp;
titular species. While most of these dinosaurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.400
are descendants of the maniraptoran group, Kosemen&nbsp;
and Roy have also populated this hypothetical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:04.800
world with various species of mammals, which have&nbsp;
managed to grow into large and sophisticated forms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.760
just like in our time, despite the presence of&nbsp;
dinosaurs. Due to the butterfly effect, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:17.120
these mammals would of course be different from&nbsp;
species we’re familiar with. One entertaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:23.360
example of a new species is the Megatapirus, a&nbsp;
large herbivore that superficially resembles an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:29.600
elephant due to the honing force of convergent&nbsp;
evolution. A neat idea. The authors also imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.360
a variety of mammalian carnivores that somewhat&nbsp;
resemble canine predators. These mammals are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.360
far from the largest organisms patrolling this&nbsp;
hypothetical world, which still very much belongs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:47.840
to the dinosaurs. On the subject of dinosaurs, the&nbsp;
authors wanted pterosaurs to fill various niches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.720
Since these creatures were already in decline&nbsp;
by the time of the asteroid’s impact, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:05:00.560
Kosemen and Roy have imagined them dwindling down&nbsp;
to a few subspecies – comparable to storks. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:07.360
terms of larger dinosaurs, we have the aptly-named&nbsp;
classification the Avititans — massive, lumbering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:13.040
herbivores that are the largest new organisms&nbsp;
on this alternate earth. Another large species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:19.600
is the Bull-Bird, a wary and dangerous scavenger&nbsp;
that stalks the plains of the Eurasian continent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:25.440
Kosemen and Roy imagine that hunting Bull-Birds&nbsp;
was a rite of passage for early dinosauroids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.160
who need to coordinate with each other to take&nbsp;
the beasts down. Some hunters enlisted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:36.480
help of jackal-birds — hawk-like species that the&nbsp;
dinosauroids, inventively, managed to domesticate:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.760
just like early humans and the ancestor of&nbsp;
the modern dog. But dinosauroids and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:48.640
domestic pets aren’t the only hunters on the&nbsp;
plains. These are the tyrannoraptors — a species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:56.400
of evolved theropods whose evolution echoes&nbsp;
that of the massive-jawed Tyrannosaurus Rex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:02.480
This dangerous predator is known by the&nbsp;
Dinosaroids as “black thing” – and are the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:09.040
predators in this imagined dominion. In order to&nbsp;
survive in this dangerous world, Dinosauroids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:15.200
the creators speculate, have had to rely on their&nbsp;
technological cunning much like early humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.880
Here, you can see a Dinosauroid with a carved&nbsp;
stone tool in its mouth used for hunting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.600
At this point in the speculative timeline,&nbsp;
most tribes of Dinosauroids are nomadic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:31.440
building temporary structures while migrating for&nbsp;
protection from the elements. One detail I like is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:36.480
that these structures look a lot like the nests&nbsp;
of modern birds — which, don’t forget, are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.560
descendants of therapods. The authors imagine that&nbsp;
the Dinosauroids do eventually form more permanent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.000
village-like settlements, which are intriguingly&nbsp;
still made up of nest-houses — just more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.000
sturdy ones. In these more permanent societies,&nbsp;
early forms of more intricate cultural expression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.760
might emerge — like this young artist painting&nbsp;
on stretched animal skins using ground up soil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.600
other natural pigments, and a brush made&nbsp;
from a wing feather. Pretty clever. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:11.600
many Dinosauroids maintain migratory lifestyles&nbsp;
— like these two individuals with a domesticated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.400
form of the bull-bird mentioned previously,&nbsp;
which is carrying their various supplies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:21.120
Other Dinosauroids travel light — like this&nbsp;
‘mountaineering’ duo carrying weapons and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:26.160
travel gear themselves. Curiously, Roy and&nbsp;
Kosemen mention their weapons are crafted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:32.720
from bronze — indicating the Dinosauroids&nbsp;
are entering a new phase of development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.680
Many groups also seem to equip themselves&nbsp;
with weaponized ‘war-masks’ — wooden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:43.280
helmet-like structures with obsidian blade edges&nbsp;
to improve the striking power of their beaks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.880
While these masked-warriors look imposing, Kosemen&nbsp;
and Roy imagine these masks, while intimidating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:54.880
aren’t the most practical. But Dinosauroids&nbsp;
society is clearly becoming more technologically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:00.880
proficient — inventing armaments like a unique&nbsp;
spear throwing device and a primitive bronze age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:08.160
set of armor. Clearly Dinosauroids are, for better&nbsp;
or worse, following our own path in many ways. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:14.320
warfare isn’t the only aspect of Dinosauroid&nbsp;
culture Kosemen and Roy have fleshed out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:20.000
The two have also created various images depicting&nbsp;
what paintings by Dinosauroids might look like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:26.880
Take, for example, this painting depicting an&nbsp;
avititan family. Here, the broad, impressionistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.000
strokes that are a trademark of the Dinosauroids’&nbsp;
style are reflective of how they use feathers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.080
apply paint. Or take this stylized painting of&nbsp;
a Dinosauroid hunter spearing some four-legged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.280
mammal — an image which reminds me of early&nbsp;
cave paintings our human ancestors created.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:48.400
Even more stylized, we have a mural of&nbsp;
a warrior confronting a spirit-creature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:53.920
Here, you can see elements of myth and legends&nbsp;
sneaking their way into the art — just as was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.480
the case with human cultural development.&nbsp;
The dangerous, predatory “black thing” gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:03.920
a similar treatment — with the organism’s mouth&nbsp;
and horns exaggerated in the artist’s recreation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:08.240
reflecting the parts of the animal that&nbsp;
stood out the most to the Dinosauroid artist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:14.400
And beyond paintings, perhaps the Dinosauroids&nbsp;
might also tell spoken stories in their own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:21.040
bird-like languages. Here, you see a fireside chat&nbsp;
between an elder and several hatchlings. Kosemen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:26.880
and Roy imagine this storyteller is weaving&nbsp;
tales of spirits and other worlds — in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:34.000
a legend of a sky-god, which resembles a flying&nbsp;
pterosaur. What a creative level of worldbuilding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.960
Beyond these early societies, who knows what&nbsp;
the future might have held for a species like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:44.640
the Dinosauroids. Perhaps their technology would&nbsp;
continue to develop — like in this hypothetical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:50.320
sketch of a more heavily armored warrior which&nbsp;
almost resembles a knight. And who knows, perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:55.600
long into a hypothetical future, this species&nbsp;
might even develop the technology necessary to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:01.680
become a space faring civilization. It’s a pretty&nbsp;
interesting concept, although not one Kosemen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:08.080
Roy have said they’ve pursued seriously. Still,&nbsp;
the hypothetical future of Dinosauroids seems full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.520
of endless potential — although we’ve reached the&nbsp;
end of our exploration for this particular video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.880
If you liked this world, please consider&nbsp;
supporting Roy and Kosemen by patronizing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.760
other works and social media, which as always you&nbsp;
can find in the links below. And if you liked this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:29.520
video, I have videos covering the speculative work&nbsp;
of C.M. Koseman elsewhere in the archive. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.480
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,&nbsp;
please lend your support and like, subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:45.840
and hit the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

